WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.500
Usually a document is just, you know, it's harmless.

00:02.940 --> 00:06.620
Like you read a legal contract and the ink just sits there on the page.

00:06.700 --> 00:08.100
It describes a rule for you.

00:08.200 --> 00:08.900
Yeah, exactly.

00:09.020 --> 00:12.060
Or you look at a painting and it just shows you a scene.

00:12.260 --> 00:17.540
I mean, our entire relationship with the written word is based on this expectation of passivity.

00:17.900 --> 00:18.720
It's just a description.

00:18.980 --> 00:19.200
Right.

00:19.620 --> 00:24.740
We like our descriptions of the world to be neatly separated from the physical mechanics of the world itself.

00:24.920 --> 00:26.360
You know, you can always just close the book.

00:26.360 --> 00:30.800
But what if a document didn't just describe a rule?

00:31.080 --> 00:34.260
What if the text physically built the walls and locked the doors?

00:34.660 --> 00:37.680
Well, I mean, it fundamentally changes what it means to write, doesn't it?

00:37.780 --> 00:38.020
Yeah.

00:38.140 --> 00:41.980
You are no longer just writing a description of an action.

00:42.080 --> 00:51.300
You're actually constructing a rigid structural boundary that the universe, or well, at least the computational universe, literally cannot disobey.

00:51.480 --> 00:51.900
Exactly.

00:52.540 --> 00:54.380
So welcome to this deep dive.

00:54.380 --> 01:00.880
Today, we are looking at a computational language where punctuation is not just a grammatical suggestion.

01:01.220 --> 01:03.440
It is a physical law.

01:03.680 --> 01:04.780
A literal physical law.

01:05.000 --> 01:05.200
Right.

01:05.680 --> 01:10.220
We're exploring a 2026 paper by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

01:10.740 --> 01:16.580
It's titled Following the Evaluator, an ASCII language for bounded state transition.

01:16.780 --> 01:17.500
It's quite a title.

01:17.660 --> 01:18.340
It is, yeah.

01:18.340 --> 01:26.540
And the mission of this deep dive for you listening today is to explore a total ground-up rethinking of programming languages.

01:27.140 --> 01:28.880
I mean, think about punctuation for a second.

01:29.520 --> 01:33.260
We normally use commas and parentheses just to group text together, right?

01:33.320 --> 01:34.980
Just so we can read it without getting confused.

01:35.040 --> 01:36.400
Yeah, just visual aids, basically.

01:36.560 --> 01:39.140
But Flyxion asks a completely different question here.

01:39.140 --> 01:48.140
What if punctuation actively controlled, restricted, and audited what a computer could physically do at any given microsecond?

01:48.140 --> 01:52.960
And I should say right up front, the source material we are pulling from today is a very dense essay.

01:53.080 --> 01:53.520
Oh, yeah.

01:53.740 --> 01:54.200
Very, yes.

01:54.300 --> 02:01.760
You glance at the original PDF, you were just immediately hit with a wall of Greek letters, logical proofs, transition relations.

02:01.940 --> 02:04.260
It looks like high-level theoretical physics or something.

02:04.420 --> 02:05.120
It really does.

02:05.260 --> 02:05.420
Yeah.

02:05.480 --> 02:09.540
But beneath all those equations, Flyxion is actually writing about something deeply human.

02:09.880 --> 02:12.640
It's about how we represent reality, you know?

02:12.640 --> 02:17.280
How we define an action, and ultimately how we record history itself.

02:17.780 --> 02:27.200
So my goal here is to translate that formal math into the underlying mechanism so we can really understand why this changes our relationship with software entirely.

02:27.440 --> 02:34.020
Well, let's start with how we currently think about code, because the mind shift really has to start with something as basic as a parenthesis.

02:34.160 --> 02:34.420
Sure.

02:34.540 --> 02:41.100
Because in most programming languages, or even just in basic middle school math, right, a parenthesis is just a grouping symbol.

02:41.100 --> 02:42.440
It's a way to organize things.

02:42.440 --> 02:45.800
Right, like, take PEMDAS, please excuse my dear Aunt Sally.

02:45.980 --> 02:47.340
We all remember that, right?

02:48.000 --> 02:48.960
Unfortunately, yes.

02:49.140 --> 02:58.820
So if you have 3 plus 4 inside parentheses, and then you multiply that by 2, the parentheses are just there to resolve the ambiguity of the sentence.

02:58.960 --> 03:00.060
They just tell you what to do first.

03:00.120 --> 03:00.560
Exactly.

03:01.000 --> 03:02.300
They tell you to do the addition first.

03:02.440 --> 03:06.720
And then, once you've done it, those parentheses just completely vanish from your mind.

03:06.820 --> 03:08.300
They disappear completely.

03:08.300 --> 03:14.420
And, you know, in computer science, this vanishing act actually happens inside what we call the parser.

03:14.640 --> 03:15.880
Okay, unpack that for us.

03:16.320 --> 03:21.820
So when you write a piece of code, the computer doesn't actually execute the exact text you typed.

03:22.200 --> 03:29.400
It reads your text, and it builds this internal model, a sort of blueprint, which we call an abstract syntax tree, or an AST.

03:29.540 --> 03:30.360
Right, the AST.

03:30.360 --> 03:37.900
Yeah, and those commas, semicolons, and parentheses that you typed out, they were really just scaffolding to help the compiler build that tree.

03:38.060 --> 03:39.120
Just temporary support.

03:39.220 --> 03:39.600
Exactly.

03:40.040 --> 03:43.440
Once the tree is built, the punctuation marks are literally thrown in the trash.

03:43.960 --> 03:46.060
They do not exist when the program actually runs.

03:46.160 --> 03:49.600
Okay, but Flyxion proposes the exact opposite discipline here.

03:49.660 --> 03:50.640
Completely the opposite.

03:50.640 --> 04:01.380
In this experimental language, every single primitive symbol must correspond to a distinct physical restriction on what the machine running the code is allowed to do.

04:01.500 --> 04:01.800
Yes.

04:01.920 --> 04:03.600
So the punctuation doesn't vanish.

04:03.740 --> 04:05.380
It actually becomes the environment.

04:05.620 --> 04:05.800
Right.

04:05.980 --> 04:11.280
And to understand the origin of this idea, the paper points back to an old teaching tool used for LISP.

04:11.540 --> 04:12.320
Okay, LISP.

04:12.320 --> 04:19.180
Yeah, so LISP is a family of programming languages that dates all the way back to the late 1950s, actually, created by John McCarthy.

04:19.600 --> 04:21.240
It's ancient in computer years.

04:21.380 --> 04:22.020
It really is.

04:22.460 --> 04:26.180
And LISP is famous for its incredibly heavy use of nested parentheses.

04:27.280 --> 04:31.640
Like, code in LISP just looks like a giant sea of curved brackets.

04:31.740 --> 04:33.440
Just walls of parentheses.

04:33.560 --> 04:34.000
Pretty much.

04:34.180 --> 04:41.380
And when professors teach nested expressions to beginners, they often use a visual aid called the circles of evaluation.

04:41.380 --> 04:41.980
Right.

04:42.360 --> 04:47.400
Instead of writing parentheses, you literally draw circles inside of circles on a whiteboard.

04:47.540 --> 04:53.000
So if you have a math problem inside a math problem, it's basically just a small circle inside a bigger circle.

04:53.160 --> 04:53.520
Exactly.

04:53.900 --> 04:56.860
And the rule of these circles is incredibly strict.

04:57.540 --> 05:04.020
To evaluate the outermost circle, the computer must first conceptually step inside the inner circle.

05:04.140 --> 05:04.460
Okay.

05:04.640 --> 05:09.960
As a solve whatever is in there, reduce it down to a single value, and then, you know, hand that value back outward.

05:10.060 --> 05:10.960
Right, I see.

05:10.960 --> 05:16.080
You literally cannot solve the outer layer until the inner layer has fully collapsed into an answer.

05:16.080 --> 05:21.840
So imagine punctuation not as grammatical markings on a page, but as physical rooms in a building.

05:22.060 --> 05:22.640
I like that.

05:22.920 --> 05:30.660
Because in a normal language, like, say, Python or JavaScript, a parenthesis is like a line drawn on the floor with tape, you know?

05:30.760 --> 05:32.080
Yeah, just a visual suggestion.

05:32.180 --> 05:35.360
Right, a visual suggestion of where the kitchen ends and the dining room begins.

05:35.420 --> 05:37.040
You can easily just step right over it.

05:37.040 --> 05:38.040
You can ignore it completely.

05:38.460 --> 05:43.300
But in flictions language, a parenthesis is a heavy physical vault door.

05:43.560 --> 05:43.880
Yes.

05:43.980 --> 05:45.640
You actually have to open it and walk through it.

05:45.680 --> 05:51.520
And once you are inside that specific room, you are entirely bound by the physics of that room.

05:51.580 --> 05:53.120
You're trapped in there until you finish the job.

05:53.220 --> 05:53.600
Exactly.

05:53.780 --> 05:55.280
You have to do the work inside.

05:55.280 --> 05:59.300
And you can only walk back out the door carrying the final result.

05:59.620 --> 06:02.180
That physical visualization is incredibly helpful.

06:02.340 --> 06:02.440
Yeah.

06:02.700 --> 06:11.560
However, I do want to note that the paper is also very careful to prevent us from mistaking our metaphors for the literal truth of the machine.

06:11.760 --> 06:12.300
Oh, sure.

06:12.600 --> 06:13.100
Good point.

06:13.260 --> 06:13.400
Yeah.

06:13.760 --> 06:17.020
Flyxion explicitly separates the design into three distinct levels.

06:17.020 --> 06:19.920
First, you have the concrete syntax.

06:20.320 --> 06:20.740
Which is what?

06:21.240 --> 06:22.880
The literal ASCII text.

06:23.200 --> 06:25.720
You know, the actual characters you type on your mechanical keyboard.

06:25.800 --> 06:26.500
Okay, got it.

06:26.680 --> 06:28.600
Second, you have the abstract syntax.

06:28.940 --> 06:31.140
That is the structural tree we talked about a minute ago.

06:31.200 --> 06:31.760
The AST.

06:32.080 --> 06:32.320
Right.

06:32.680 --> 06:34.800
And third, you have the semantics.

06:35.000 --> 06:37.340
That is the actual operational machine transitions.

06:37.900 --> 06:41.120
The microscopic flipping of bits in the processor and memory.

06:41.120 --> 06:47.260
Meaning words like enter or follow and return, those are just our evocative human metaphors.

06:47.520 --> 06:48.280
Yes, exactly.

06:48.400 --> 06:50.720
They belong to our reading of the situation, right?

06:50.720 --> 06:50.840
Right.

06:50.840 --> 06:51.980
Not the code itself.

06:52.100 --> 06:57.780
Right, because Flyxion explicitly writes that no claim is made that computation is spatial.

06:58.300 --> 07:01.020
The computer isn't literally walking into a room.

07:01.140 --> 07:01.500
Exactly.

07:01.600 --> 07:03.660
It's not walking into a geometric sphere or anything.

07:03.820 --> 07:10.200
When we say entering a sphere, the mathematical counterpart to that is a formal property called traversal decomposition.

07:10.200 --> 07:11.120
Okay, wait.

07:11.480 --> 07:14.540
What does traversal decomposition actually mean for the physical machine?

07:15.240 --> 07:22.700
It simply means that the formal math proves the evaluator must fully resolve the inner term before it can resolve the outer context.

07:22.960 --> 07:24.800
So it breaks it down into strict stats.

07:24.820 --> 07:26.300
Yes, it decomposes the journey.

07:26.380 --> 07:32.500
And keeping these three levels separate, the text, the tree, and the machine semantics, is crucial here.

07:32.760 --> 07:33.780
Why is that so important?

07:33.780 --> 07:41.240
Because a lot of modern language design gets really muddy when creators let one level silently define another.

07:41.640 --> 07:49.140
Like, they'll draw a nice visual flowchart and mistake their illustrative drawing for a formal definition of how the memory should behave.

07:49.280 --> 07:49.880
Oh, I see.

07:49.980 --> 07:51.960
They confuse the map for the territory.

07:52.100 --> 07:52.460
Exactly.

07:53.020 --> 07:55.620
And Flyxion refuses to make that mistake.

07:55.620 --> 08:02.600
Okay, so if punctuation acts like a physical room with strict rules, what are those rules?

08:02.720 --> 08:04.060
Like, how many rooms are there?

08:04.160 --> 08:08.160
Well, this brings up Flyxion's core design principle, which is called motion economy.

08:08.440 --> 08:09.380
Let's unpack that.

08:09.560 --> 08:10.400
Motion economy.

08:10.580 --> 08:12.560
Yeah, let's break down the two halves of this principle.

08:12.660 --> 08:13.800
The first half is economy.

08:14.600 --> 08:20.900
In mathematically precise terms, no two primitive symbols can denote the same machine motion.

08:21.020 --> 08:22.140
Meaning no overlaps.

08:22.140 --> 08:22.500
Right.

08:22.960 --> 08:24.440
Think of a steering wheel in a car.

08:25.120 --> 08:27.180
Turning it left means the car goes left.

08:27.320 --> 08:27.780
Simple enough.

08:28.040 --> 08:33.960
You don't have three different buttons, a lever, and a weird dial on the dashboard that also make the car go left.

08:34.140 --> 08:35.660
That would be a nightmare to drive.

08:35.760 --> 08:36.300
It would be.

08:36.760 --> 08:40.600
There is a strict one-to-one mapping between the command and the motion.

08:40.940 --> 08:41.860
Okay, and the second half?

08:42.020 --> 08:43.700
The second half is completeness.

08:43.820 --> 08:44.080
Mm-hmm.

08:44.260 --> 08:49.340
Meaning for every core motion the machine is allowed to make, there must be a declared symbol for it.

08:49.360 --> 08:50.600
There are no hidden actions.

08:50.600 --> 08:51.720
Okay, hold on.

08:52.000 --> 08:57.020
Let me push back on this a little because I'm trying to put myself in the shoes of a working software engineer right now.

08:57.180 --> 08:57.640
Fair enough.

08:57.720 --> 09:07.420
You're saying this is revolutionary, but JavaScript, Python, C++, I mean, they already have keywords that do specific things, right?

09:07.520 --> 09:08.080
Sure they do.

09:08.160 --> 09:11.260
You have if statements, you have while loops, you have return keys.

09:11.740 --> 09:18.100
Why do we need an entirely new alphabet of ASI symbols just to do what basic code already does?

09:18.100 --> 09:20.280
It is a totally valid skepticism.

09:20.720 --> 09:24.020
But let's look at what actually happens in modern programming languages.

09:24.700 --> 09:26.700
They are incredibly bloated.

09:26.960 --> 09:27.740
Bloated how?

09:28.060 --> 09:33.800
Well, a language like C++ has nearly 100 reserved keywords, and JavaScript has dozens.

09:34.300 --> 09:38.220
And the problem is, many of these keywords overlap in functionality.

09:38.480 --> 09:40.680
Oh, like multiple buttons turning the car left.

09:41.020 --> 09:41.420
Exactly.

09:41.420 --> 09:45.540
Or worse, they perform multiple hidden state changes under the hood.

09:46.200 --> 09:56.120
For example, a single keyword in a modern language might allocate memory on the heap, check a Boolean condition, and mutate a variable all at the exact same time.

09:56.440 --> 09:56.740
Wow.

09:57.140 --> 09:59.400
Okay, so one word does a bunch of invisible stuff.

09:59.500 --> 09:59.640
Right.

09:59.640 --> 10:03.800
The programmer types one word, but the machine is doing five different things.

10:04.380 --> 10:08.100
Flyxion's constraint absolutely prevents this kind of semantic drift.

10:08.200 --> 10:08.820
It's about parity.

10:09.320 --> 10:09.640
Yes.

10:10.300 --> 10:20.880
By forcing a strict one-to-one relationship between a single ASCII symbol and a single pure machine motion, the language is kept tiny, but mathematically verifiable.

10:20.880 --> 10:23.500
And when you say tiny, I mean, you mean seven symbols.

10:23.660 --> 10:24.100
Just seven.

10:24.220 --> 10:26.000
That is the entire core alphabet.

10:26.620 --> 10:27.820
Let's walk through these symbols.

10:27.940 --> 10:30.300
I want to know exactly what buttons we have on this dashboard.

10:30.400 --> 10:30.820
Let's do it.

10:30.960 --> 10:34.720
So first, you have the standard parentheses, just an open and closed bracket.

10:34.840 --> 10:35.000
Right.

10:35.080 --> 10:37.320
And this represents pure nested evaluation.

10:38.120 --> 10:42.000
It's the basic enter and return we talked about with the circles of evaluation.

10:42.260 --> 10:42.460
Okay.

10:42.840 --> 10:47.300
It defines the region of work, but importantly, it doesn't change the state of the world outside of it.

10:47.540 --> 10:47.960
Got it.

10:48.660 --> 10:50.840
Then we get to the real meat of the paper.

10:51.040 --> 10:52.320
The bounded transition frame.

10:52.500 --> 10:52.740
Yes.

10:52.940 --> 10:53.380
The big one.

10:53.560 --> 10:59.740
This is written as a less than sign, a parenthesis, and then on the other side, a parenthesis and a greater than sign.

10:59.820 --> 11:01.780
So it looks like a sort of spiked parenthesis.

11:01.900 --> 11:01.980
Yeah.

11:02.080 --> 11:03.900
Think of that symbol as the heavy vault door.

11:04.320 --> 11:08.180
It delimits a frame with what the paper calls a staged store.

11:08.480 --> 11:09.840
A staged store.

11:10.080 --> 11:11.580
What does that mean in normal terms?

11:12.080 --> 11:17.100
It basically creates an isolated sandbox where you can play around without affecting reality.

11:17.140 --> 11:17.320
Okay.

11:17.320 --> 11:17.920
That makes sense.

11:18.260 --> 11:19.420
Next is the question mark.

11:19.420 --> 11:21.260
This is the admit motion.

11:21.360 --> 11:22.500
It's a perspective test.

11:22.640 --> 11:23.900
Then you have the tilde symbol.

11:24.080 --> 11:25.400
This is the transform motion.

11:25.780 --> 11:31.100
It represents a pure change made only inside that isolated sandbox, the staged store.

11:31.100 --> 11:37.680
And notice how the question mark and the tilde map strictly to entirely different machine motions here.

11:37.820 --> 11:38.020
Right.

11:38.100 --> 11:40.500
Because one asks a question and the other does an action.

11:40.580 --> 11:40.960
Exactly.

11:41.400 --> 11:48.800
One asks a question of the state without touching it, and the other forcefully changes a temporary state without asking any questions at all.

11:48.800 --> 11:49.320
Okay.

11:49.640 --> 11:51.040
Then we have the exclamation point.

11:51.160 --> 11:51.320
Sure.

11:51.360 --> 11:52.680
This is the verify motion.

11:52.680 --> 11:55.940
It's a relational check that happens after a change is made.

11:56.280 --> 11:56.440
Yeah.

11:56.520 --> 11:58.960
Following that is the at symbol, which is attribute.

11:59.620 --> 12:03.860
This constructs the provenance or the authorship of the action.

12:04.140 --> 12:05.840
Like, who is actually doing this?

12:05.880 --> 12:07.360
It stamps the identity on it.

12:07.360 --> 12:11.660
And finally, the greater than sign, the commit motion.

12:12.060 --> 12:17.240
This is the only symbol that actually makes a change permanent in the real world.

12:17.240 --> 12:17.640
Right.

12:17.900 --> 12:24.860
And, you know, the paper tests its own principle of motion economy against its own design choices in a really transparent way.

12:25.000 --> 12:25.340
Oh, so.

12:25.340 --> 12:35.780
Well, for instance, in an earlier draft, Flyxion asked, why do we need both a question mark to admit and an exclamation point to verify?

12:36.080 --> 12:36.280
Yeah.

12:36.400 --> 12:37.460
Actually, why do we?

12:37.520 --> 12:39.680
Aren't they both just testing if something is allowed?

12:39.840 --> 12:41.040
That's exactly the question.

12:41.220 --> 12:45.200
If I want to check if a user has enough money in their account, isn't a check just a check?

12:45.280 --> 12:45.540
Right.

12:45.680 --> 12:48.400
But the answer is they have fundamentally different mathematical signatures.

12:48.580 --> 12:48.780
Okay.

12:48.900 --> 12:49.880
Lay that out for us.

12:49.880 --> 12:56.300
The question mark admission predicate only looks at the committed store meaning, the past, and the proposal you're making.

12:57.060 --> 12:59.820
It evaluates before any transformation is actually run.

12:59.920 --> 13:01.700
So it's looking before you leap.

13:01.980 --> 13:02.300
Yes.

13:02.720 --> 13:08.960
It asks, based on the state of the world right now, are you even allowed to attempt this math?

13:09.380 --> 13:14.040
Oh, like checking if the account is frozen before you even try to calculate the withdrawal.

13:14.200 --> 13:14.620
Exactly.

13:14.620 --> 13:22.580
Now, the exclamation point verification predicate, on the other hand, reads the committed store, the staged store, and the proposed effects.

13:22.760 --> 13:23.720
So it looks at everything.

13:23.880 --> 13:24.080
Right.

13:24.400 --> 13:33.020
It asks, now that you've done the math in the sandbox, does the relationship between the before state and the after state satisfy our required invariance?

13:33.420 --> 13:34.980
Did the math actually check out?

13:35.220 --> 13:35.660
I see.

13:35.660 --> 13:46.060
So admission is perspective, verification is relational, there are entirely different epistemic positions, so the principle of motion economy mandates they absolutely must have completely different symbols.

13:46.180 --> 13:46.520
Wow.

13:46.900 --> 13:55.340
Okay, so we have this tiny seven-symbol alphabet, but like if I just throw a commit symbol next to a transform symbol, wouldn't that instantly break the isolation we just talked about?

13:55.620 --> 13:56.320
It would, yes.

13:56.440 --> 13:59.480
So how does the language prevent me from writing a dangerous sentence?

14:00.120 --> 14:04.020
That is where the grammar of the language becomes a literal, physical barrier.

14:04.020 --> 14:06.780
Flyxion doesn't just give you the alphabet.

14:07.160 --> 14:10.420
There is a rigid sequence you absolutely must follow.

14:10.600 --> 14:11.640
Okay, what's the sequence?

14:12.200 --> 14:16.660
The paper defines the static well-formedness of the language.

14:17.700 --> 14:22.680
A bounded transition is only valid if it matches exactly this regular expression sequence.

14:23.360 --> 14:32.480
Any number of admits, followed by any number of transforms, followed by at least one verify, followed by at least one attribute, followed by exactly one commit.

14:32.480 --> 14:35.780
Okay, let's strip away the computer science terminology for a second here.

14:35.820 --> 14:36.000
Sure.

14:36.200 --> 14:39.700
That means you can ask zero or more questions with the question mark.

14:40.040 --> 14:43.380
Then you can make zero or more isolated changes with the tilde.

14:43.680 --> 14:43.960
Right.

14:44.160 --> 14:48.620
Then you absolutely must have at least one exclamation point to verify the math.

14:48.620 --> 14:51.540
Then at least one at symbol to stamp who did it.

14:51.840 --> 14:54.960
And finally, exactly one greater than sign to make it real.

14:55.180 --> 14:55.460
Yes.

14:55.660 --> 15:00.840
Admit, transform, verify, attribute, commit in that exact order.

15:00.980 --> 15:02.860
And this ordering is a static judgment.

15:03.060 --> 15:04.580
Wait, what does static judgment mean?

15:04.920 --> 15:08.500
It means it's checked by the parser before the code ever runs.

15:08.500 --> 15:13.460
The machine won't even attempt to turn on the engine if the sequence is wrong.

15:13.540 --> 15:14.860
It just refuses to start.

15:15.000 --> 15:15.400
Exactly.

15:15.820 --> 15:21.820
It enforces a vital mathematical property called boundary locality, which is Proposition 5 in the paper.

15:21.940 --> 15:22.200
Okay.

15:22.480 --> 15:24.320
Walk us through boundary locality.

15:24.660 --> 15:31.320
So when you open that bounded transition with the vault door symbols, you are creating a localized environment.

15:31.320 --> 15:39.000
Inside this boundary, any changes you make with the tilde transform symbol are only applied to a staged store.

15:39.180 --> 15:40.360
Which is like a scratch pad.

15:40.480 --> 15:40.680
Yes.

15:40.820 --> 15:43.940
Think of a staged store as a scratch pad in the computer's memory.

15:44.920 --> 15:52.220
The committed store, the actual permanent database of the machine and the outside world that remains completely untouched and isolated.

15:52.400 --> 15:53.920
It doesn't even know you're doing math.

15:53.980 --> 15:54.180
Right.

15:54.180 --> 16:03.680
Only at the very end, if every single verification passes, does the commit motion take that scratch pad and atomically overwrite the permanent database.

16:03.920 --> 16:08.380
Let me use an analogy here to ground this because I think it's one of the most powerful concepts in the text.

16:08.440 --> 16:11.540
And I really want to make sure you, the listener, grasp this.

16:11.640 --> 16:12.060
Let's hear it.

16:12.280 --> 16:14.960
Think of a high-end Michelin star restaurant.

16:15.540 --> 16:19.360
The vault door boundary is the physical walls of the kitchen itself.

16:19.500 --> 16:19.720
Okay.

16:19.780 --> 16:20.640
I'll look where this is going.

16:20.640 --> 16:24.360
And the tilde transform motion is the chef in the back at the prep station.

16:24.600 --> 16:30.540
They are experimenting with a new dish, chopping ingredients, mixing sauces, maybe burning a pan or two.

16:30.980 --> 16:31.760
Doing the messy work.

16:31.840 --> 16:32.040
Right.

16:32.160 --> 16:34.280
This is all happening in the staged store.

16:35.080 --> 16:42.900
Out in the dining room, which is the committed store, the real world, the guests have absolutely no idea this is happening.

16:43.440 --> 16:45.100
Their reality hasn't changed at all.

16:45.540 --> 16:49.640
The dining room remains perfectly pristine, quiet, and orderly.

16:49.640 --> 16:50.680
Right.

16:50.780 --> 16:54.420
So the chef's messy counter does not impact the customer's clean table.

16:54.540 --> 16:54.940
Exactly.

16:55.320 --> 16:59.720
Now, the chef finishes plating the dish, but they can't just run it out to the dining room themselves.

16:59.840 --> 17:02.380
It has to go to the pass, where the head chef stands.

17:02.500 --> 17:04.400
And the head chef is the verification stage.

17:04.600 --> 17:04.900
Yes.

17:05.380 --> 17:08.820
The head chef performs the exclamation point verify motion.

17:09.240 --> 17:10.740
They taste a spoon of the sauce.

17:10.860 --> 17:12.100
They wipe the rim of the plate.

17:12.280 --> 17:13.280
They check the presentation.

17:13.280 --> 17:17.720
They are verifying the relationship between the raw ingredients and the final plate.

17:18.180 --> 17:19.780
They're checking the math, essentially.

17:20.480 --> 17:25.540
And if, and only if, the head chef God approves the dish, they ring the bell.

17:25.780 --> 17:27.280
That bell is the commit motion.

17:27.860 --> 17:28.640
Ah, perfect.

17:28.640 --> 17:31.380
The waiter carries the dish out through the swinging doors.

17:32.020 --> 17:35.400
And only then does the reality of the dining room change.

17:35.740 --> 17:38.940
That captures the essence of boundary locality perfectly.

17:39.520 --> 17:41.180
Because the interior can mutate.

17:41.300 --> 17:41.840
It can fail.

17:41.960 --> 17:43.280
It can change state wildly.

17:43.680 --> 17:47.620
But the exterior does not change until that commit surface is officially crossed.

17:47.760 --> 17:48.000
Right.

17:48.000 --> 17:53.000
And this is why the strict ordering of the symbols is not just some stylistic choice.

17:53.560 --> 17:55.700
It is absolutely mandatory for safety.

17:55.860 --> 17:59.240
If you mess with the order, the safety guarantees completely collapse.

17:59.680 --> 18:04.680
Flyxion provides these brilliant negative examples in the paper to prove exactly why this

18:04.680 --> 18:05.780
order matters, actually.

18:05.920 --> 18:06.840
Yeah, section 10, right.

18:06.920 --> 18:07.360
Exactly.

18:07.860 --> 18:10.780
I want to throw a couple of these at you to see how the system handles it.

18:11.220 --> 18:15.340
What if a programmer tries to be clever and writes their code out of order?

18:15.340 --> 18:18.640
Like, what if they put the commit symbol before the verify symbol?

18:18.760 --> 18:23.360
Well, mathematically, that breaks Proposition 8, which guarantees verify commits.

18:23.760 --> 18:26.140
But practically, what does that look like?

18:26.540 --> 18:31.300
In your kitchen analogy, that is the waiter running the dish out to the customer, the customer

18:31.300 --> 18:35.800
taking a bite, chewing it, and then the head chef coming out to taste it to see if it's

18:35.800 --> 18:36.380
cooked properly.

18:36.580 --> 18:37.300
Oh, wow.

18:37.600 --> 18:38.520
Yeah, that's terrible.

18:38.700 --> 18:39.780
It is far too late.

18:40.000 --> 18:41.180
The damage is already done.

18:41.180 --> 18:45.600
You are trying to check the invariant after the permanent state of the world has already

18:45.600 --> 18:46.040
been altered.

18:46.380 --> 18:48.140
You can't uneat the bad food.

18:48.460 --> 18:48.880
Exactly.

18:49.580 --> 18:54.800
So by enforcing static well-formedness, the parser rules this out immediately.

18:55.260 --> 18:58.860
It is physically impossible to execute that sequence in this language.

18:59.200 --> 19:00.680
Here is another negative example.

19:01.240 --> 19:03.660
What if we admit after we transform?

19:04.220 --> 19:07.360
So we write a tilde transform and then a question mark admit.

19:07.640 --> 19:09.760
So changing the state before checking the rules.

19:09.760 --> 19:10.020
Right.

19:10.160 --> 19:14.880
So we chop all the vegetables, cook the steak, and then check if the customer actually ordered

19:14.880 --> 19:15.380
steak.

19:15.760 --> 19:19.820
If a language permitted arbitrary effects, this would be completely disastrous.

19:20.520 --> 19:25.160
A program could make a change, potentially an irreversible effect, like sending an email,

19:25.580 --> 19:30.100
triggering an alarm, or transferring funds out of a bank account before checking if the

19:30.100 --> 19:31.880
user actually had the permission to do so.

19:31.900 --> 19:33.780
Which is how so many bugs happen today.

19:33.900 --> 19:34.180
Right.

19:34.180 --> 19:40.280
But by forcing all admission tests to occur strictly before any transformations, the language

19:40.280 --> 19:45.500
guarantees that no work is done, and no state is mutated even on the scratch pad, unless

19:45.500 --> 19:47.520
the perspective conditions are met first.

19:47.940 --> 19:48.940
It's so elegant.

19:49.540 --> 19:54.140
This strict sequence ensures the outside world is entirely protected from the staging area.

19:54.220 --> 19:54.880
It really is.

19:54.880 --> 19:57.280
But this raises a massive question.

19:58.000 --> 20:02.760
What happens if the head chef tastes the dish during the verify stage and just spits it

20:02.760 --> 20:03.000
out?

20:03.200 --> 20:03.960
A total rejection.

20:04.180 --> 20:04.360
Right.

20:04.540 --> 20:06.640
What happens if the transition hits a refusal?

20:07.200 --> 20:09.100
Does the dish just vanish into the ether?

20:09.320 --> 20:10.880
Does the system pretend it never happened?

20:11.100 --> 20:16.180
This is where Flyxion's design moves from just being, you know, a safe programming language

20:16.180 --> 20:19.600
to being a profound statement on how we record history.

20:19.740 --> 20:19.960
Okay.

20:19.960 --> 20:20.920
Let's get into it.

20:21.240 --> 20:25.300
The paper defines three possible outcomes for any bounded transition.

20:26.120 --> 20:28.860
First, it can commit, which we've already discussed.

20:29.300 --> 20:30.060
The bell rings.

20:30.240 --> 20:31.080
The dish goes out.

20:31.160 --> 20:32.040
Everything goes perfectly.

20:32.240 --> 20:32.380
Right.

20:32.780 --> 20:38.240
Second, it can refuse, meaning a predicate like an admit or verify test returned false.

20:38.800 --> 20:40.240
The head chef rejected it.

20:40.300 --> 20:40.520
Okay.

20:40.600 --> 20:42.460
Or third, it can fault.

20:42.640 --> 20:45.560
Wait, what is the difference between a refusal and a fault?

20:45.780 --> 20:47.260
Aren't they both just errors?

20:47.760 --> 20:48.400
Not at all.

20:48.400 --> 20:53.760
A fault means the evaluation couldn't be completed at all due to a breakdown in the physical

20:53.760 --> 20:55.740
or logical reality of the machine.

20:56.000 --> 20:56.540
Give me an example.

20:56.820 --> 21:01.560
Maybe a variable was completely undefined or the hard drive crashed or the system literally

21:01.560 --> 21:02.320
ran out of memory.

21:02.600 --> 21:04.060
Oh, so the kitchen caught on fire.

21:04.180 --> 21:04.460
Exactly.

21:04.760 --> 21:06.120
The chef didn't reject the dish.

21:06.260 --> 21:07.720
The kitchen just ceased to function.

21:08.040 --> 21:08.240
Okay.

21:08.300 --> 21:10.380
So how does the language handle these outcomes?

21:10.740 --> 21:14.280
That is governed by what the paper calls ledger legality.

21:14.440 --> 21:15.220
Ledger legality.

21:15.260 --> 21:15.560
Yes.

21:15.820 --> 21:17.020
Proposition six and seven.

21:17.020 --> 21:21.540
Behind the scenes, the evaluator maintains a ledger of everything that happens.

21:22.160 --> 21:25.360
And this ledger is appenned only in the strongest mathematical sense.

21:25.660 --> 21:27.860
It uses a property called prefix extension.

21:28.180 --> 21:29.180
Prefix extension.

21:29.180 --> 21:32.620
That sounds like a concept from cryptography or, you know, blockchains.

21:32.740 --> 21:34.160
It is heavily used there, yeah.

21:34.340 --> 21:38.240
But without all the crypto hype, it's really just a simple logical rule.

21:38.380 --> 21:38.540
Yeah.

21:38.540 --> 21:39.780
History cannot be rewritten.

21:39.900 --> 21:40.120
Okay.

21:40.280 --> 21:45.040
Whatever was in the ledger 10 steps ago is still the exact prefix of the ledger now.

21:45.120 --> 21:45.300
Yeah.

21:45.300 --> 21:46.920
You can only add to the end of the book.

21:47.140 --> 21:50.940
You can never tear out a page or erase a line or go back in time.

21:50.940 --> 21:56.320
There is a mathematical formula in the text that absolutely blew my mind regarding this ledger.

21:56.580 --> 22:02.480
It says, delta sigma equals zero, but delta lambda does not equal zero.

22:02.700 --> 22:03.040
Yes.

22:03.240 --> 22:04.160
That's a crucial one.

22:04.280 --> 22:07.140
Let me translate that out of the Greek for a second, just so we're all on the same page.

22:07.560 --> 22:09.400
Delta means change, right?

22:09.600 --> 22:12.640
Sigma is the state of the world, the committed store, the dining room.

22:12.640 --> 22:16.260
And lambda is the ledger, the logbook of history.

22:16.440 --> 22:16.680
Correct.

22:16.840 --> 22:21.760
So the change in the state of the world is zero, but the change in the ledger is not zero.

22:22.120 --> 22:28.840
If we connect this to the bigger picture, it is a profound realization about how systems should work.

22:29.500 --> 22:32.260
No state change does not mean no event occurred.

22:32.500 --> 22:37.660
Let's break that down because it goes completely against how almost all modern databases work today.

22:37.760 --> 22:38.100
It does.

22:38.380 --> 22:41.260
Imagine a transition hits an emit test and the test fails.

22:41.260 --> 22:46.200
Let's say a user tries to withdraw money from an ATM, but they top the wrong password.

22:46.300 --> 22:46.440
Okay.

22:47.060 --> 22:47.960
Happens all the time.

22:48.340 --> 22:49.840
The transition returns a refuse.

22:50.740 --> 22:55.240
Now, because of boundary locality, the world state, sigma, has not changed.

22:55.780 --> 22:57.100
Delta sigma is zero.

22:57.340 --> 22:58.460
No money was transferred.

22:58.760 --> 22:59.020
Right.

22:59.360 --> 23:01.320
The bank account remains exactly the same.

23:01.680 --> 23:06.240
But the ledger, lambda, records that the refusal happened.

23:06.340 --> 23:06.880
Oh, I see.

23:07.160 --> 23:08.540
Delta lambda is not zero.

23:08.540 --> 23:11.640
The logbook physically grows to document the rejection.

23:12.400 --> 23:14.760
A negative event is still a historical event.

23:14.960 --> 23:17.860
That is so counterintuitive to how we usually think about computing.

23:18.320 --> 23:22.100
Usually if a transaction fails in a regular SQL database, it just rolls back.

23:22.200 --> 23:22.780
It disappears.

23:23.040 --> 23:23.580
Yeah, it's a ghost.

23:23.700 --> 23:25.080
It's like it never happened at all.

23:25.360 --> 23:34.040
But Flyxion argues that a refused proposal leaves the world unchanged, yet leaves concrete evidence that the evaluation was attempted.

23:34.040 --> 23:36.500
Think about why this matters.

23:37.100 --> 23:42.780
If someone tries to guess your password 10,000 times and fails, the state of your bank account hasn't changed.

23:42.980 --> 23:43.360
Right.

23:43.760 --> 23:49.460
But wouldn't you want the historical record to show that someone attempted to breach your account 10,000 times?

23:49.700 --> 23:50.220
Absolutely.

23:50.380 --> 23:51.780
I'd want to know that immediately.

23:51.780 --> 23:57.360
And importantly, the ledger explicitly differentiates between a refusal and a fault.

23:58.320 --> 24:03.320
A refusal is a deliberate, logical decision made by the system based on its rules.

24:03.780 --> 24:04.980
The password was wrong.

24:05.220 --> 24:06.340
The system did its job.

24:06.420 --> 24:06.680
Yes.

24:07.200 --> 24:08.300
A fault is a crash.

24:08.780 --> 24:10.360
The database server lost power.

24:10.360 --> 24:13.840
These are conceptually different historical events.

24:14.420 --> 24:20.120
One is a system acting correctly to reject invalid input, and the other is a system failing to act at all.

24:20.360 --> 24:22.540
And they are recorded differently in the Lambda ledger.

24:22.640 --> 24:22.960
Exactly.

24:23.440 --> 24:25.600
They have completely different footprints in the history book.

24:25.640 --> 24:30.060
Which means an auditor can't confuse a deliberate rejection with a simple timeout error.

24:30.420 --> 24:31.780
The history is perfectly preserved.

24:31.780 --> 24:43.340
And, you know, this realization that a refusal is a real, tangible, historical event, even if it doesn't change the state of the world, it leads directly into the philosophical core of the paper.

24:43.740 --> 24:45.220
The epistemology of computation.

24:45.380 --> 24:45.620
Yes.

24:45.740 --> 24:47.040
The epistemology of computation.

24:47.220 --> 24:50.660
Now, epistemology is just a fancy word for the study of how we know what we know.

24:50.840 --> 24:51.280
Pretty much.

24:51.380 --> 24:51.540
Yeah.

24:51.740 --> 25:01.080
And section 17 of the paper is essentially a list of concepts that ordinary human description and standard programming languages casually mash together.

25:01.080 --> 25:02.280
Why do they match them together?

25:02.480 --> 25:03.320
Because it's easier.

25:03.880 --> 25:06.980
They conflate distinct ideas to save time or memory.

25:07.680 --> 25:11.860
But Flyxion explicitly refuses to merge these concepts.

25:12.540 --> 25:15.840
The language mathematically forces us to keep them separate.

25:16.020 --> 25:18.600
I really want to focus on these pairings because they are fascinating.

25:18.760 --> 25:22.180
The paper outlines several of these non-collapse principles.

25:22.480 --> 25:23.100
Let's go through them.

25:23.160 --> 25:24.020
Let's start with the first one.

25:24.100 --> 25:25.600
Evaluation versus exposure.

25:25.920 --> 25:30.000
Basically, just because a computer calculates something doesn't mean the world sees it.

25:30.000 --> 25:33.140
We touched on this with the staging area, but it's really worth expanding.

25:33.860 --> 25:44.160
In a normal language, like, say, JavaScript on a web page, if a function calculates a value and assigns it to a global variable, it is immediately exposed.

25:44.320 --> 25:45.300
Everyone can see it.

25:45.520 --> 25:45.660
Right.

25:46.360 --> 25:48.580
Evaluation and exposure happen simultaneously.

25:49.360 --> 25:51.060
Flyxion forces a wedge between them.

25:51.060 --> 26:04.880
Inside the boundary, you can evaluate thousands of complex transformations, you know, run algorithms, crunch numbers, but absolutely none of it is exposed to the committed world until that explicit commit symbol is executed.

26:05.180 --> 26:07.540
Computing something is not the same as publishing it.

26:07.720 --> 26:08.160
Exactly.

26:08.340 --> 26:10.500
Modern web developers mess this up constantly.

26:10.640 --> 26:16.680
You see websites where half a page loads, an error happens, and you are left looking at a broken, half-computed state.

26:16.740 --> 26:17.480
We've all seen it.

26:17.480 --> 26:21.140
It's terrible, and it happens because they collapse evaluation and exposure.

26:21.300 --> 26:21.500
Right.

26:21.780 --> 26:22.960
Okay, here's the next pair.

26:23.620 --> 26:25.260
Proposal versus realization.

26:25.700 --> 26:27.700
This builds on our discussion of refusals.

26:28.000 --> 26:32.620
A proposal can be fully evaluated without ever being realized in the committed store.

26:32.900 --> 26:34.220
But we conflate them anyway.

26:34.220 --> 26:38.120
In everyday life, we make this category error all the time.

26:39.180 --> 26:44.220
We treat the representation of a possibility as the execution of that possibility.

26:44.300 --> 26:45.340
Give me an example of that.

26:45.340 --> 26:49.120
If I hand you a blueprint for a house, that is a proposal.

26:49.760 --> 26:50.820
It can be evaluated.

26:51.340 --> 26:54.980
You can check if the load-bearing walls hold up, if the plumbing makes sense, whatever.

26:55.180 --> 26:56.820
But it is not a realized house.

26:57.060 --> 26:58.160
You can't live in a blueprint.

26:59.380 --> 27:05.580
Flyxion's language tracks proposals as distinct entities from realized state changes.

27:06.000 --> 27:07.720
A stage store is just a blueprint.

27:07.720 --> 27:09.940
It's so logical when you lay it out like that.

27:10.120 --> 27:13.520
Let's hit the third pair, which we've circled around but really need to nail down.

27:13.780 --> 27:15.740
Admission versus verification.

27:16.060 --> 27:16.760
Ah, yes.

27:16.840 --> 27:18.280
I want to use a nightclub analogy here.

27:18.380 --> 27:20.260
Admission, the question mark symbol, is perspective.

27:20.780 --> 27:24.120
It is the bouncer standing at the velvet rope outside the club.

27:24.240 --> 27:25.560
Okay, I can picture that.

27:25.640 --> 27:28.760
The bouncer checks your ID based on what is known before you enter.

27:28.880 --> 27:29.500
Are you 21?

27:29.820 --> 27:30.880
Are you wearing dress shoes?

27:31.060 --> 27:32.140
Are you on the guest list?

27:32.560 --> 27:34.140
It is a test of the pre-state.

27:34.140 --> 27:38.280
It is a forward-looking gatekeeper, has no idea what you will do inside the club.

27:38.760 --> 27:41.760
It only knows if you meet the criteria to open the door.

27:42.060 --> 27:45.240
But verification, the exclamation point symbol is relational.

27:45.440 --> 27:46.120
It's not the bouncer.

27:46.320 --> 27:49.080
It's the auditor checking the books after the night is over.

27:49.360 --> 27:49.640
Yes.

27:50.100 --> 27:54.660
The auditor looks at the cash register before the night started and after the night ended

27:54.660 --> 27:56.680
to ensure the invariance hold.

27:57.420 --> 28:02.040
Did every single drink poured result in exactly $14 entering the till?

28:02.040 --> 28:04.180
And this raises a really important question.

28:05.140 --> 28:09.360
Why do so many systems conflate the bouncer and the auditor?

28:09.820 --> 28:10.360
Why do they?

28:10.740 --> 28:14.420
Because it is computationally cheaper and conceptually lazier.

28:14.740 --> 28:18.100
It's easier to just do one check and assume everything is fine.

28:18.420 --> 28:22.240
But they occupy fundamentally different epistemic positions.

28:22.460 --> 28:22.700
Right.

28:22.800 --> 28:24.240
The bouncer only knows the past.

28:24.580 --> 28:27.840
The auditor knows the relationship between the past and the proposed future.

28:27.840 --> 28:33.640
Flyxion mathematically forces you to use different symbols for these actions, because confusing

28:33.640 --> 28:35.000
them leads to illegal histories.

28:35.080 --> 28:36.580
Which is exactly what we want to avoid.

28:36.840 --> 28:37.080
Right.

28:37.560 --> 28:42.140
In a world of information overload, we constantly collapse these concepts in our daily lives.

28:42.640 --> 28:45.680
We'd assume because a law was proposed, it happened.

28:46.120 --> 28:48.880
Or because a policy was evaluated, it was exposed.

28:49.780 --> 28:54.880
This language is essentially a masterclass in using syntax to enforce epistemic hygiene.

28:55.220 --> 28:56.180
Epistemic hygiene.

28:56.780 --> 28:57.700
That is brilliant.

28:58.700 --> 29:02.400
And speaking of things we falsely collapse, let's talk about the difference between an

29:02.400 --> 29:04.140
event and the record of an event.

29:04.580 --> 29:07.360
What is actually going into this ledger we keep talking about?

29:07.640 --> 29:12.040
We're entering the territory of Proposition 8, Verified Commit, and Proposition 9, Witness

29:12.040 --> 29:12.840
Correspondence.

29:12.960 --> 29:13.700
Heavy hitters.

29:13.960 --> 29:15.340
Yeah, the paper makes a hard distinction.

29:16.040 --> 29:17.120
An event is not a witness.

29:17.560 --> 29:19.960
The physical transition of the state is the event.

29:20.420 --> 29:22.420
The ledger entry that records it is the witness.

29:22.420 --> 29:25.580
And the rule here is conservation of committed history.

29:25.880 --> 29:26.120
Yes.

29:26.540 --> 29:31.200
It states that the number of commit steps mathematically must equal the number of valid witness records

29:31.200 --> 29:31.800
in the ledger.

29:32.100 --> 29:33.800
You cannot have one without the other.

29:33.920 --> 29:37.660
The paper has this great visual breakdown about orphans that I really want to mention.

29:38.060 --> 29:42.020
Because in a lot of corporate databases, you can have what Flyxion calls a left orphan.

29:42.420 --> 29:43.140
A left orphan.

29:43.480 --> 29:43.720
Right.

29:43.960 --> 29:47.520
That is a state change that happened, but there is no witness record of it.

29:47.520 --> 29:50.780
It's a silent mutation, a back door.

29:51.360 --> 29:53.000
It's terrifying from a security standpoint.

29:53.520 --> 29:53.600
Yeah.

29:53.660 --> 29:58.340
Imagine someone going into the SQL database, changing their account balance from zero to

29:58.340 --> 30:02.060
a million dollars, and totally bypassing the logging system.

30:02.260 --> 30:03.100
That's a left orphan.

30:03.760 --> 30:06.020
And on the flip side, you can have a right orphan.

30:06.600 --> 30:10.680
That is a witness record asserting that a change happened, but the actual state change

30:10.680 --> 30:11.860
never occurred in reality.

30:11.940 --> 30:13.600
It's a hallucinated log entry.

30:13.600 --> 30:14.040
Exactly.

30:14.540 --> 30:18.700
Like, a log says an email was sent, but the server was actually offline and the email

30:18.700 --> 30:19.240
never went out.

30:19.360 --> 30:24.540
But because of how Flyxion's core calculus is structured, with the commit motion being

30:24.540 --> 30:29.620
a single atomic step that both updates the store and appends the witness simultaneously,

30:30.100 --> 30:33.500
both left orphans and right orphans are mathematically impossible.

30:33.880 --> 30:37.460
Every single commit appends exactly one witness.

30:38.120 --> 30:40.940
Every single witness corresponds to exactly one commit.

30:41.260 --> 30:42.820
It's a perfect one-to-one ratio.

30:42.820 --> 30:43.980
It's airtight.

30:44.380 --> 30:45.540
It guarantees perfect ordering.

30:46.440 --> 30:52.500
But, and this is a massive caveat that Flyxion highlights in the text, persistence does not

30:52.500 --> 30:53.320
equal truth.

30:54.060 --> 30:57.160
This is perhaps the most critical thinking point in the entire text.

30:57.500 --> 30:58.180
It really is.

30:58.280 --> 30:58.840
Why is that?

30:59.140 --> 31:05.220
Because it is so easy to look at an immutable blockchain or a perfectly conserved append-only

31:05.220 --> 31:09.400
ledger and just assume that because the record is permanent and mathematically flawless,

31:09.720 --> 31:12.400
the content of the record must be objectively true.

31:12.400 --> 31:14.260
Right, because it's set in stone.

31:14.380 --> 31:17.260
But Flyxion explicitly warns against this.

31:18.040 --> 31:19.680
Persist does not imply true.

31:20.020 --> 31:21.040
So what does it imply?

31:21.200 --> 31:22.760
If it's not true, what is it?

31:23.000 --> 31:25.600
A valid witness only certifies structural facts.

31:25.820 --> 31:26.600
Structural facts.

31:26.760 --> 31:27.000
Yes.

31:27.280 --> 31:32.020
It proves that this specific transition ran, starting from this specific pre-state hash,

31:32.340 --> 31:36.560
ending at this specific post-state hash, and that all the declared predicates returned

31:36.560 --> 31:38.140
a true Boolean value.

31:38.140 --> 31:40.840
So it proves the procedural structure was followed perfectly.

31:41.040 --> 31:41.300
Exactly.

31:41.560 --> 31:46.620
But if the predicate itself was written poorly like, if it returns true when it shouldn't,

31:46.700 --> 31:51.740
or if the data fed into it was a lie, the witness will dutously persist that flawed outcome.

31:52.000 --> 31:53.240
It doesn't judge the data.

31:53.360 --> 31:53.580
Right.

31:53.920 --> 31:55.920
It proves the system followed its own rules.

31:56.060 --> 31:56.160
Yeah.

31:56.160 --> 31:59.760
It does not prove the rules accurately reflect objective reality.

31:59.860 --> 32:03.940
It's like the difference between a legally binding contract and a fair contract.

32:04.180 --> 32:05.260
Oh, that's a good way to put it.

32:05.340 --> 32:11.340
Like, if I get a contract notarized, the notary stamp just proves that I am the person who signed it on that specific date.

32:11.600 --> 32:13.400
It proves the structure of the agreement.

32:13.720 --> 32:17.580
It doesn't prove that the contract isn't a terrible deal that completely ruins my life.

32:17.640 --> 32:17.980
Exactly.

32:18.160 --> 32:23.400
The notary is a witness to the event, not a guarantor of universal truth.

32:23.400 --> 32:28.200
So a witness preserves the structure of an event, but it isn't the event itself.

32:28.980 --> 32:34.120
Flyxion provides a really compelling real-world analogy in the paper to help us grasp this concept.

32:34.280 --> 32:38.140
It addresses the non-collapse principle that representation is not referent.

32:38.280 --> 32:38.480
Right.

32:38.740 --> 32:41.320
Imagine you are sending a YouTube link to a friend.

32:41.720 --> 32:45.900
You paste a 52-character string of assy, text, text-like,

32:46.280 --> 32:53.000
https.www.youtube.com forward slash vvv, followed by some random letters.

32:53.000 --> 32:54.080
We all know the format.

32:54.500 --> 32:59.160
When you send those 52 bytes of text, you are not sending them the actual video.

32:59.780 --> 33:06.440
The video is gigabytes of high-definition visual data stored on a massive server rack in a data center somewhere in Oregon.

33:06.900 --> 33:12.440
And even if you append a timestamp to the URL, like T86, to jump to a specific second,

33:12.980 --> 33:17.940
that string of text still does not contain the physical event happening at that second.

33:18.280 --> 33:20.620
So what exactly is the URL then?

33:20.620 --> 33:24.560
The paper dissects this by separating three preservation targets.

33:24.680 --> 33:25.500
Okay, let's hear them.

33:25.620 --> 33:27.200
First, object preservation.

33:28.220 --> 33:32.340
That is saving the actual video file itself, storing the gigabytes of data.

33:32.440 --> 33:33.000
The heavy lifting.

33:33.240 --> 33:33.440
Right.

33:33.820 --> 33:37.780
Second, representation preservation, saving the exact characters of the URL.

33:38.440 --> 33:40.700
And third, reachability preservation.

33:41.540 --> 33:44.800
The URL is still resolved to the video when you click it five years from now.

33:44.800 --> 33:47.040
Oh man, we all know the pain of link decay.

33:47.420 --> 33:50.780
You click a bookmark from 10 years ago and you just get a 404 error.

33:51.020 --> 33:51.940
The server rotted.

33:52.140 --> 33:52.480
Exactly.

33:52.640 --> 33:53.980
A doorway can fail to resolve.

33:54.100 --> 33:57.920
You can have a perfectly preserved URL, meaning flawless representation preservation.

33:58.480 --> 34:00.740
But if the server goes down, you've lost reachability.

34:00.900 --> 34:02.660
So the representation is just a doorway.

34:02.660 --> 34:09.940
Yes, a representation is a preserved possibility of traversal, not a guarantee of arrival.

34:10.040 --> 34:12.480
What does this all mean for this ASCII language though?

34:12.560 --> 34:18.140
Like why is this YouTube URL analogy in a paper about bounded state transitions?

34:18.540 --> 34:22.920
Because the witness artifact, the ledger record created when a transition commits,

34:23.220 --> 34:25.740
is exactly like that URL doorway.

34:25.900 --> 34:26.660
Okay, I'm tracking.

34:26.780 --> 34:28.840
It doesn't contain the actual transition itself.

34:28.840 --> 34:33.920
The physical transition was a fleeting microscopic moment of computational time

34:33.920 --> 34:36.360
that vanished the millisecond it finished.

34:36.540 --> 34:37.400
Right, it's already gone.

34:37.980 --> 34:40.420
But the witness preserves enough structural information,

34:40.560 --> 34:42.600
the hashes of the before and after states,

34:42.920 --> 34:44.180
the predicates that were checked,

34:44.480 --> 34:46.480
the cryptographic signature of the author.

34:47.100 --> 34:49.980
It saves all of that so another evaluator can check it later.

34:50.100 --> 34:50.620
Oh, I see.

34:50.760 --> 34:52.140
It is a traversable specification.

34:52.780 --> 34:55.780
It allows you to reconstruct the exact path of history

34:55.780 --> 35:00.860
without having to physically store the entire state of the universe at every single step.

35:00.860 --> 35:02.460
That is incredibly efficient.

35:03.220 --> 35:05.740
And the ultimate form of preserving this representation

35:05.740 --> 35:11.300
brings us back to the very specific choice of characters Flyxion use to build this system.

35:11.880 --> 35:15.300
We need to talk about why this is an ASCII language.

35:15.880 --> 35:20.100
Not graphical nodes, not visual blocks, but raw text.

35:20.100 --> 35:23.260
We really need to look at Proposition 1 from the paper for this.

35:24.080 --> 35:27.620
It establishes unique parsing and a canonical round trip.

35:28.060 --> 35:28.700
Let me guess.

35:28.880 --> 35:29.720
More math.

35:29.940 --> 35:30.420
Just a little.

35:31.060 --> 35:35.140
The mathematical formula is print, parenthesis, parts, parenthesis, s,

35:35.340 --> 35:37.740
close parenthesis, close parenthesis, equals s.

35:37.840 --> 35:39.940
Okay, print of parse of s equals s.

35:40.000 --> 35:42.900
What does that actually mean in practice for someone typing out code?

35:43.300 --> 35:46.120
It means that if you take a string of source code, let's call it s,

35:46.260 --> 35:49.440
and you parse it into that abstract syntax tree we talked about earlier.

35:49.440 --> 35:50.260
Right, the blueprint.

35:50.840 --> 35:51.260
Exactly.

35:51.640 --> 35:55.620
And then you immediately tell the compiler to print that tree back out into characters,

35:55.880 --> 35:58.400
you get the exact original string s back.

35:58.840 --> 35:59.700
Every single space.

36:00.040 --> 36:00.920
Every single bracket.

36:01.080 --> 36:02.060
It's perfectly symmetrical.

36:02.480 --> 36:04.040
It is a perfect mathematical identity.

36:04.160 --> 36:05.160
But why does this matter?

36:05.340 --> 36:08.400
Like, why should a programmer care if it prints back exactly the same?

36:08.600 --> 36:11.040
Because a spatially suggestive notation,

36:11.480 --> 36:14.400
like the circles of evaluation or nested boxes

36:14.400 --> 36:18.060
or the visual node-based programming you see in modern game engines,

36:18.060 --> 36:23.000
it usually requires a massive, complex graphical user interface to render.

36:23.120 --> 36:23.300
Right.

36:23.380 --> 36:25.900
You need specific software to even open the file.

36:26.020 --> 36:26.580
Exactly.

36:26.740 --> 36:26.900
Yeah.

36:26.960 --> 36:31.520
But Flyxion restricted this language entirely to printable ASCII characters.

36:31.760 --> 36:34.200
Because the physical layout of the text,

36:34.460 --> 36:36.940
the indentation, the nesting of the normal parenthesis,

36:37.060 --> 36:37.980
and the vault door parenthesis,

36:38.440 --> 36:41.080
is mathematically identical to the execution structure.

36:41.540 --> 36:43.200
This code doesn't need a rendering layer.

36:43.200 --> 36:43.760
Wow.

36:44.080 --> 36:46.020
So it survives minimal terminals.

36:46.300 --> 36:48.520
It survives plain text editors like Notepad.

36:48.660 --> 36:51.280
It survives archival version control diffs.

36:51.360 --> 36:52.780
It survives practically everything.

36:52.820 --> 36:55.120
Like, if the entire modern operating system collapses,

36:55.240 --> 36:58.060
you can still read and verify the structure of this code

36:58.060 --> 37:00.160
on a 1980s monochrome monitor.

37:00.260 --> 37:00.600
Exactly.

37:00.820 --> 37:03.140
The visual structure is the computational structure.

37:03.660 --> 37:06.080
You don't need special software to see the boundaries.

37:06.220 --> 37:07.820
The plain ASCII characters are the boundaries.

37:08.020 --> 37:09.040
That is so cool.

37:09.040 --> 37:10.880
But if you synthesize this whole system,

37:11.480 --> 37:14.720
what Flyxion has built is a system of monotone narrowing.

37:14.860 --> 37:15.860
Monotone narrowing.

37:15.920 --> 37:17.380
Let's define that for everyone listening.

37:17.940 --> 37:21.180
Each layer you pass through acts as a restrictive filter.

37:21.820 --> 37:23.040
You start at the outer parenthesis.

37:23.360 --> 37:24.640
You enter the bounded frame.

37:25.020 --> 37:26.380
You pass the admission tests.

37:26.760 --> 37:28.400
You work through the stage transformations.

37:28.820 --> 37:30.060
You pass the verifications.

37:30.360 --> 37:32.220
And finally, you exit through the commit door.

37:32.440 --> 37:33.620
Like a series of airlocks.

37:34.040 --> 37:34.340
Yes.

37:34.340 --> 37:37.080
Each layer filters the possibilities,

37:37.500 --> 37:40.360
monotonically narrowing down the potential states of chaos

37:40.360 --> 37:43.500
until you are left with a mathematically pure,

37:44.020 --> 37:45.660
perfectly audited state change.

37:46.300 --> 37:48.280
It is just an incredible piece of work.

37:48.620 --> 37:50.820
It takes the absolute smallest components

37:50.820 --> 37:52.480
of writing punctuation marks

37:52.480 --> 37:54.820
and turns them into a fortress of logic.

37:54.980 --> 37:55.620
It really does.

37:55.880 --> 37:57.700
Let's summarize the journey we've just been on

37:57.700 --> 37:59.440
because we covered a lot of ground today.

37:59.720 --> 38:02.280
We started with the simple idea of Lisk circles,

38:02.280 --> 38:05.540
the idea that punctuation could do more than just group text.

38:06.040 --> 38:08.620
From there, we explored Flyxion's mathematically sound

38:08.620 --> 38:10.180
seven-symbol ASCII alphabet.

38:10.480 --> 38:10.740
Right.

38:10.940 --> 38:12.960
A language governed by motion economy,

38:13.180 --> 38:15.480
where every symbol acts as a physical doorway

38:15.480 --> 38:17.360
restricting what the machine can do,

38:17.840 --> 38:21.180
completely preventing the bloat and overlap of modern coding.

38:21.180 --> 38:23.980
We saw how enforcing a strict static sequence,

38:24.260 --> 38:28.020
admit, transform, verify, attribute commit,

38:28.580 --> 38:31.080
creates a perfectly isolated staging area,

38:31.260 --> 38:34.180
which protects the real world from unverified changes.

38:34.400 --> 38:36.180
We dove into the immutable ledger,

38:36.400 --> 38:38.280
learning that even a refused proposal

38:38.280 --> 38:41.220
is a real historical event that must be recorded.

38:41.380 --> 38:41.720
Yes.

38:42.100 --> 38:44.960
And we explored those crucial non-collapse principles,

38:45.380 --> 38:48.020
reminding us not to confuse a proposal with a realization,

38:48.020 --> 38:50.020
or the bouncer with the auditor,

38:50.480 --> 38:52.420
or persistence with objective truth.

38:52.900 --> 38:55.680
And finally, we saw how the restriction to plain ASCII

38:55.680 --> 38:58.340
ensures that the visual representation of the code

38:58.340 --> 39:00.700
is perfectly tied to its execution structure.

39:01.260 --> 39:04.440
It acts as a permanent, traversable doorway to history

39:04.440 --> 39:07.360
that doesn't rely on complex graphics to survive.

39:07.360 --> 39:10.360
It's a brilliant synthesis of syntax and semantics.

39:10.660 --> 39:12.500
It is a massive amount of theory to process,

39:12.720 --> 39:14.660
but it fundamentally changes how you look

39:14.660 --> 39:15.880
at the structure of information.

39:15.880 --> 39:18.260
Now, before we sign off, I want to leave you,

39:18.400 --> 39:20.520
the listener, with a final provocative thought

39:20.520 --> 39:21.220
to mull over.

39:21.720 --> 39:23.580
Something that builds on this source material,

39:23.920 --> 39:26.000
but takes it completely out of the realm of computers.

39:26.420 --> 39:27.200
Oh, I'm intrigued.

39:27.380 --> 39:28.820
Think about our opening.

39:29.340 --> 39:32.600
The idea of a document that isn't just descriptive ink,

39:32.660 --> 39:34.180
but structural walls.

39:35.040 --> 39:37.280
If Flyxion can create an ASCII system

39:37.280 --> 39:39.460
where the text itself inherently proves

39:39.460 --> 39:40.980
its own structural history,

39:41.380 --> 39:44.000
where the grammar makes illegal histories

39:44.000 --> 39:45.680
physically impossible to execute,

39:46.180 --> 39:49.180
imagine applying the principle of motion economy

39:49.180 --> 39:52.160
and bounded transitions to human systems.

39:52.160 --> 39:53.780
That is a staggering leap.

39:53.900 --> 39:56.480
I mean, human systems are famously unbound.

39:56.700 --> 39:57.060
They are.

39:57.200 --> 39:57.680
They're messy.

39:58.000 --> 39:59.620
But what if our legal contracts,

39:59.780 --> 40:00.800
our corporate policies,

40:00.960 --> 40:02.560
or even our political legislations

40:02.560 --> 40:03.980
were written in a language

40:03.980 --> 40:05.260
where the grammar itself

40:05.260 --> 40:06.600
made it physically impossible

40:06.600 --> 40:07.820
to execute a change

40:07.820 --> 40:09.640
without a structurally valid,

40:09.920 --> 40:10.900
unorphaned witness?

40:11.040 --> 40:11.460
Oh, wow.

40:11.460 --> 40:14.000
What if a CEO literally could not mutate

40:14.000 --> 40:16.040
a corporate-staged store, say,

40:16.340 --> 40:17.980
moving millions in company funds

40:17.980 --> 40:19.260
without passing a strict,

40:19.740 --> 40:21.160
verifiable admission test

40:21.160 --> 40:23.640
built into the very syntax of their authority?

40:23.760 --> 40:25.520
That would change corporate governance overnight.

40:25.860 --> 40:27.460
What if a law couldn't be enacted

40:27.460 --> 40:29.140
unless the relational verification

40:29.140 --> 40:30.740
of its economic impact

40:30.740 --> 40:31.920
was statically checked

40:31.920 --> 40:33.200
before the final commit?

40:34.160 --> 40:35.540
How would society change

40:35.540 --> 40:37.300
if our laws weren't just guidelines

40:37.300 --> 40:38.520
we hoped people followed,

40:38.660 --> 40:39.580
but were actual,

40:39.820 --> 40:41.720
executable, bounded transitions?

40:42.220 --> 40:44.080
It moves the burden of compliance

40:44.080 --> 40:45.860
from post-event auditing

40:45.860 --> 40:48.400
to pre-event physical impossibility.

40:48.700 --> 40:49.060
Exactly.

40:49.580 --> 40:50.460
It's something to think about

40:50.460 --> 40:51.780
the next time you sign a contract,

40:52.100 --> 40:52.900
read a piece of code,

40:52.980 --> 40:53.300
or, you know,

40:53.380 --> 40:55.340
just look at a parenthesis on a page.

40:55.760 --> 40:57.160
Thank you so much for joining us

40:57.160 --> 40:58.440
on this intellectual deep dive.

40:58.640 --> 40:59.320
We hope it gave you

40:59.320 --> 41:00.280
the perspective shift

41:00.280 --> 41:01.060
you were looking for.

41:01.360 --> 41:02.560
Keep questioning the boundaries.

